#edb/ref 1061 131207 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186143646 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "af52054c-35d9-57c3-98d7-5a48f6962dda" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186143646 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186144073 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186143647 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186143648 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186143647 #edb/ref 1061 131208 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186143647 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f1026160-5385-56a6-8797-842c1e0a9527" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186143647 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186142782 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186143648 #edb/ref 1061 131211 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186143648 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5fc59a74-e40e-569f-af5a-bd734121e5e4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186143648 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186142783 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186143666 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186143667 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186143666 #edb/ref 1061 135462 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186143666 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "34b8df92-1cb5-5d85-b9c3-c28c62faa4f4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186143666 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186144091 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186143667 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186143668 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186143667 #edb/ref 1061 135463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186143667 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ad2aa659-63dd-584a-b088-2ba77cc88124" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186143667 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186142795 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186143668 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186143669 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186143668 #edb/ref 1061 135466 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186143668 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2c4252d5-5840-55f8-ba2c-0227c7321d1a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186143668 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186142796 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186143669 #edb/ref 1061 135469 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186143669 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e62b1b5f-b518-59e2-b361-2cc41f5b3e7b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186143669 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186142797 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186144071 #edb/ref 1064 "To solve an expression that contains parentheses, $(\\,)$, we follow the **order of operations with parentheses**. This means that we calculate the operation inside parentheses first, from left to right, before following the standard order of operations.\n\nFor example, let's use the order of operations with parentheses to evaluate $30 \\div (5 \\times 2)$.\n\nFirst, we perform the operation inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n30 \\div (5 \\cdot 2) &=  \\\\\n&= 30 \\div 10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n30 \\div 10 &=  \\\\\n&= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186144071 #edb/ref 1065 8566 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186144071 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "c73a1b14-33f9-5b9c-b640-581391c33369" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186144071 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186144072 #edb/ref 1064 "Another pair of grouping symbols that are similar to parentheses are square brackets, $[\\:]$.\n\nTo solve an expression that contains both parentheses and brackets, the order of operations with parentheses tells us to:\n\n- Calculate the operations in the parentheses *first*.\n- Calculate the operations in square brackets *second*.\n- Finally, follow the order of operations, as usual.\n\nTo illustrate, let's evaluate the following expression:\n\n$$\n1+ [2 \\times (3 + 4)]\n$$\n\nFirst, we perform the operation inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 + [2 \\cdot (3 + 4)] &=  \\\\\n&= 1 + [2 \\cdot 7]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we perform the operation inside the square brackets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 + [2 \\cdot 7] &=  \\\\\n&= 1 + 14\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we add:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 + 14 &=  \\\\\n&= 15\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186144072 #edb/ref 1065 8568 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186144072 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "063858eb-73d6-58c7-9161-ad5726a4de6a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186144072 #edb/ref 1067 "Numerical Expressions With Square Brackets" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186144073 #edb/ref 1064 "Yet another pair of grouping symbols that are similar to parentheses and square brackets are braces, $\\{\\,\\}$.\n\nFor example, one expression that uses all three grouping symbols is:\n$\\{6 \\div [(3 + 5) \\div 4] \\} + 5$\n\nFor expressions containing parentheses, square brackets, and braces, the order of operations with parentheses tells us to:\n\n- Calculate the operations in the parentheses *first*.\n- Calculate the operations in the square brackets *second*.\n- Calculate the operations in braces *third*.\n- Finally, follow the order of operations, as usual.\n\nSo, to evaluate the expression above, first, we perform the operation inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n{6 \\div [(3 + 5) \\div 4]} + 5 &=  \\\\\n&= {6 \\div [8 \\div 4]} + 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we perform the operation inside the square brackets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n{6 \\div [8 \\div 4]} + 5 &=  \\\\\n&= {6 \\div 2} + 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we perform the operation inside the braces:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n{6 \\div 2} + 5 &=  \\\\\n&= 3 + 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we add:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3 + 5 &=  \\\\\n&= 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186144073 #edb/ref 1065 8571 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186144073 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "c2534ed1-72be-5d1d-a1d4-a07550442b13" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186144073 #edb/ref 1067 "Numerical Expressions With Braces" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186144091 #edb/ref 1064 "To multiply fractions, we multiply the numerators, multiply the denominators, and simplify (if necessary).\n\nFor example, to multiply $\\dfrac 1 {2} \\times \\dfrac 1 {3},$ we multiply the numerators and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{blue}1}{\\color{red}2} \\times \\dfrac{\\color{blue}1}{\\color{red}3} =  \\dfrac {{\\color{blue}1}\\times {\\color{blue}1}} {{\\color{red}2}\\times {\\color{red}3}} = \\dfrac{1}{6}\n$$\n\nNotice that we get the same answer when we use a fraction model.\n\n![](images/6d/6d4a0a382c9624510581d94e5829b2be6907e493aaba20eb471901f061b87820.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186144091 #edb/ref 1065 8927 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186144091 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "a71c2faa-d009-5288-8863-51238a73c406" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186144091 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198430 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049001 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198430 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186198987 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198430 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186198990 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198430 #edb/ref 1039 "Comparing Fractions When One Denominator Is a Multiple of the Other" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198430 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186198072 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198430 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "44fe2ae1-cc39-587f-923e-8d972a710f3b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198431 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186198988 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198431 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186198991 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198431 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186440395 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198431 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186446983 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198431 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186478459 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198431 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186485489 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198431 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186490298 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198431 #edb/ref 1039 "Comparing Fractions When the Least Common Denominator Equals the Product of Denominators" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198431 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186198073 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198431 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "bf381e5b-c6ba-5255-8d6a-797576f71baf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198432 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050444 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198432 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050581 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198432 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186198989 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198432 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186198992 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198432 #edb/ref 1039 "Comparing Fractions When the Denominators Have a Common Factor" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198432 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186198074 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198432 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "dba89cfa-b854-5e77-8ec3-a8a5387e1284" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198504 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186199364 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198504 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186199365 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198504 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186199366 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198504 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186199367 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198504 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186199385 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198504 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186199386 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198504 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198504 #edb/ref 1045 "Comparing Fractions Using Common Denominators" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198504 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186199367 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198504 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049640 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198504 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "16dc136b-432c-5dc7-a8a2-ed7099e307f8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199364 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199385 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199364 #edb/ref 1061 45210 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199364 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8f9c6e33-6295-588d-8999-777f967dd926" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199364 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186198430 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199365 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199386 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199365 #edb/ref 1061 45213 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199365 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "08ad9ff4-b6a2-5573-9122-1358d801f979" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199365 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186198431 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199366 #edb/ref 1061 45216 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199366 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "861b0ef5-57db-54f1-9e2d-753ce0381aaf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199366 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186198432 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199367 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199364 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199367 #edb/ref 1061 45243 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199367 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b29119b6-dd48-5ca3-b83d-aa49d38cb72e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199367 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186199497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199385 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199365 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199385 #edb/ref 1061 45627 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199385 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ff41765d-5768-5be7-b7c9-fc631dd1a47b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199385 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186199511 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199386 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199366 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199386 #edb/ref 1061 45628 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199386 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f04271e1-d9ba-5ac2-8cbf-99e19cd76ae0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199386 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186199512 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199497 #edb/ref 1064 "We saw how to compare fraction models using common denominators in a previous lesson. In this lesson, we'll learn how to carry out these processes without models.\n\nFor example, suppose we have the following two fractions:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}3} \\qquad \\dfrac{4}{\\color{red}6}\n$$\n\nWhich of them is larger?\n\nAt the moment, it's difficult to compare them as they don't have a common denominator. Therefore, we must *create* a common denominator.\n\nNotice that our denominators are ${\\color{blue}3}$ and ${\\color{red}{6}}$, and ${\\color{red}{6}}$ is a multiple of ${\\color{blue}3}$. In fact,\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{6}} = {\\color{purple}{2}} \\times {\\color{blue}3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can create a common denominator by multiplying the numerator and denominator of the *first* fraction by ${\\color{purple}{2}}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{3} &= \\frac{1 \\cdot 2}{3 \\cdot 2} = \\frac{2}{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we now have the following two fractions:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{\\color{red}6} \\qquad \\dfrac{4}{\\color{red}6}\n$$\n\nFrom here, it's easy to see that the second fraction is larger. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{\\color{red}6} < \\dfrac{4}{\\color{red}6}\\,\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}3} < \\dfrac{4}{\\color{red}6}\n$$\n\nLet's see another example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199497 #edb/ref 1065 7821 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199497 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "f0d25e8e-bb9e-50d9-b3d0-fd69a9943adb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199497 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199511 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the following fractions:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}2} \\qquad \\dfrac{1}{\\color{red}3}\n$$\n\nLet's compare these fractions using common denominators.\n\nNotice that ${\\color{red}3}$ is *not* a multiple of ${\\color{blue}2}$. However, we can create a common denominator by taking the product of the denominators.\n\nIn this case, our common denominator will be\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}2} \\times {\\color{red}3} = 6\n$$\n\n- To put $\\dfrac12$ over a denominator of $6$, we multiply the numerator and denominator by ${\\color{red}3}{:}$\n$\\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{1 \\cdot 3}{2 \\cdot 3} = \\frac{3}{6}$\n- To put $\\dfrac13$ over a denominator of $6$, we multiply the numerator and denominator by ${\\color{blue}2}{:}$\n$\\frac{1}{3} = \\frac{1 \\cdot 2}{3 \\cdot 2} = \\frac{2}{6}$\n\nSo, we now have the following two fractions:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac36 \\qquad\\dfrac26\n$$\n\nFrom here, it's easy to see that the first fraction is larger. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac36 > \\dfrac26\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac12 > \\dfrac13\n$$\n\nLet's see another example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199511 #edb/ref 1065 7921 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199511 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "6d0d99a2-4c43-5268-82e8-cb9f2c133f94" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199511 #edb/ref 1067 "Cases Where the Least Common Denominator Is a Product of the Denominators" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199512 #edb/ref 1064 "Whenever two denominators have a common factor greater than $1$, the most efficient way to find a least common denominator is to look at the denominators' multiples.\n\nFor example, consider the following fractions:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{\\color{blue}6} \\qquad \\dfrac{5}{\\color{red}9}\n$$\n\nNotice that ${\\color{blue}6}$ and ${\\color{red}9}$ have $3$ as a common factor. This suggests that we can create a common denominator by looking at their least common multiples:\n\n- Multiples of $6{:}\\qquad 6,12,{\\color{purple}{18}},24,\\ldots$\n- Multiples of $9{:}\\qquad 9,{\\color{purple}{18}},27,36,\\ldots$\n\nTherefore, our least common denominator will be ${\\color{purple}{18}}$.\n\n- To put $\\dfrac26$ over a denominator of $18$, we multiply the numerator and denominator by $3{:}$\n$\\frac{2}{6} = \\frac{2 \\cdot 3}{6 \\cdot 3} = \\frac{6}{18}$\n- To put $\\dfrac59$ over a denominator of $18$, we multiply the numerator and denominator by $2{:}$\n$\\frac{5}{9} = \\frac{5 \\cdot 2}{9 \\cdot 2} = \\frac{10}{18}$\n\nSo, we now have the following two fractions:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{6}{18} \\qquad\\dfrac{10}{18}\n$$\n\nFrom here, it's easy to see that the second fraction is larger. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{6}{18}< \\dfrac{10}{18}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac26 < \\dfrac59\n$$\n\nLet's see another example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199512 #edb/ref 1065 7922 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199512 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "5537959f-5b04-5412-93ab-5f1fde21a8f2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199512 #edb/ref 1067 "Using Lowest Common Multiples" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440378 #edb/ref 1080 "4\\frac{8}{9}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440378 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440378 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "186dfbec-40e1-5669-b78b-1a6e666b138c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440379 #edb/ref 1080 "20" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440379 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440379 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "48840b8b-45fc-5af4-9eaf-3b0433653b29" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440380 #edb/ref 1080 ">" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440380 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440380 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4d48c43c-8024-5436-8502-563ff420ec3c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440381 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{17}{27}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440381 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440381 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7401d26c-d5ee-5687-a7b2-679f73e8f3f1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440382 #edb/ref 1080 "504" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440382 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440382 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b1870102-803f-50cc-8c3b-5afef7cecb4e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440383 #edb/ref 1080 ">" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440383 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440383 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f46a10ec-6f4d-59dd-a288-fa100b9f0e3d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440384 #edb/ref 1080 "2\\frac{2}{5}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440384 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440384 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f7eeccef-0153-5dbc-86a4-c355fb99fe59" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440385 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186440384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440385 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440385 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440385 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186440384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440385 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "34368c67-3db4-5d2a-b4a5-7e644a1384c1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440386 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186440379 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440386 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440386 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440386 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186440379 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440386 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "5349a1c7-9dd6-5f93-9b66-8b75dd18d825" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440387 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186440378 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440387 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440387 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440387 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186440378 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440387 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "61d4181d-edef-5c2f-bb3f-ce5a078247b4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440388 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186440383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440388 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440388 #edb/ref 1077 "field-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440388 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186440383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440388 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "89dc9221-5a2e-543b-a0cb-442c6540822e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440389 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186440380 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440389 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440389 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440389 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186440380 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440389 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "8fcd58ca-53e4-51a1-8c9a-e0b485e4a754" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440390 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186440381 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440390 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440390 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440390 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186440381 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440390 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "b7069eb3-7077-523c-883d-c4e8428dace8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440391 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186440382 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440391 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440391 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440391 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186440382 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440391 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d9ab1077-8f29-5a4e-a28a-f16902739e34" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440392 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186440386 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440392 #edb/ref 1069 "q-134231" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440392 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440392 #edb/ref 1071 "Matt and Andrea each bought $6$ ounces of salted peanuts and $4$ ounces of unsalted peanuts. In total, they bought$\\,${{field-1}}\n$\\,$ounces of peanuts." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440392 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "02df687b-3c38-56ea-bcbb-7b6992abb8b8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440392 #edb/ref 1074 "We must write and evaluate an expression representing the total amount of peanuts.\n\nSince each of them bought $6$ ounces of salted peanuts and $4$ ounces of unsalted peanuts, the amount of peanuts they purchased, in ounces, is:\n\n$$\n{{6}_{⏟}}_{\\text{salted peanuts}}+{{4}_{⏟}}_{\\text{unsalted peanuts}}\n$$\n\nSince *each* person purchased this amount of peanuts, and there are $2$ people, we must multiply the amount of peanuts by $2.$ Therefore, the total amount of peanuts is:\n\n$$\n{{(6+4)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{each person}}\\times {{2}_{⏟}}_{\\text{people}}\n$$\n\nTo evaluate the expression, we follow the order of operations with parentheses.\n\nFirst, we perform the operations inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(6+4)\\times 2 & =(6+4)\\times 2 \\\\  & =10\\times 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply:\n\n$$\n10\\times 2=20\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440393 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186440385 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440393 #edb/ref 1069 "q-134235" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440393 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440393 #edb/ref 1071 "Of the $3$ pounds of walnuts that Robert bought, he gave $\\frac{3}{5}$ pounds to his granddaughter Sophia. As a mixed number, Robert has{{field-1}}\npounds of walnuts remaining." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440393 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "357eb628-a446-5e21-9526-dabfe7335132" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440393 #edb/ref 1074 "To find out how many walnuts Robert had remaining, we must calculate the difference $3-\\frac{3}{5}.$\n\nTo subtract $\\frac{3}{5}$ from $3,$ we need to express the whole number $3$ as an equivalent fraction with a denominator of $5.$\n\nFirst, we write $3$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{1}\n$$\n\nThen, we put this fraction over a denominator of $5$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $5:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{3\\times 5}{1\\times 5}=\\frac{15}{5}\n$$\n\nSo now, we have to find:\n\n$$\n\\frac{15}{5}-\\frac{3}{5}\n$$\n\nTo subtract two fractions with like denominators, we subtract the numerators and keep the denominators the same. Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\frac{15}{5}-\\frac{3}{5}=\\frac{15-3}{5}=\\frac{12}{5}\n$$\n\nFinally, we convert to a mixed number:\n\n$$\n12÷5=2\\,\\text{R}\\,2=2\\,\\frac{2}{5}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Robert had $2\\,\\frac{2}{5}$ pounds of walnuts remaining." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440394 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186440390 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440394 #edb/ref 1069 "q-134237" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440394 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440394 #edb/ref 1071 "Susan ate $\\frac{1}{3}$ of the walnuts that she bought. What **exact** amount of walnuts did she eat?\n\n*Remember that Susan bought $\\frac{17}{9}$ of a pound of walnuts.*\n\nSusan ate$\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$pounds of walnuts." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440394 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "56c0d0ac-e441-53cb-9b72-c25279d28ac0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440394 #edb/ref 1074 "We know that Susan has $\\frac{17}{9}$ of a pound of walnuts.\n\nTo calculate the amount that she ate, we need to multiply $\\frac{1}{3}$ by $\\frac{17}{9}.$\n\nTo multiply two fractions, we multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{3}\\times \\frac{17}{9}=\\frac{1\\times 17}{3\\times 9}=\\frac{17}{27}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, Susan ate $\\frac{17}{27}$ of  a pound of walnuts." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440395 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186440388 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440395 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186440389 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440395 #edb/ref 1069 "q-134236" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440395 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440395 #edb/ref 1071 "After Sophia takes her share of walnuts, Robert's remaining amount of walnuts is{{field-1}}\nthan Susan's amount of walnuts because $2\\,\\frac{2}{5}$\n{{field-2}} $\\frac{17}{9}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440395 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "994437db-5e6c-52cd-85e4-193a53ab9c7f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440395 #edb/ref 1074 "Robert has $2\\,\\frac{2}{5}$ pounds of walnuts remaining, and Susan has $\\frac{17}{9}$ pounds of walnuts.\n\nWe cannot compare the fractions directly as they don't have a common denominator.\n\nWe can make a common denominator of $5\\times 9=45.$\n\nTo put $2\\,\\frac{2}{5}$ over a denominator of $45,$ we first convert to an improper fraction and then multiply the numerator and denominator by $9:$\n\n$$\n2\\,\\frac{2}{5}=\\frac{12}{5}=\\frac{12\\times 9}{5\\times 9}=\\frac{108}{45}\n$$\n\nTo put $\\frac{17}{9}$ over a denominator of $45,$ we multiply the numerator and denominator by $5:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{17}{9}=\\frac{17\\times 5}{9\\times 5}=\\frac{85}{45}\n$$\n\nWe can now compare the fractions $\\frac{108}{45}$ and $\\frac{85}{45}$ by looking at the numerators only.\n\nSince $108>85,$ the correct statement must be:\n\n$$\n\\frac{108}{45}>\\frac{85}{45}\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is\n\n> \"After Sophia takes her walnuts, Robert's remaining amount of walnuts is $>$ than Susan's amount of walnuts because $2\\,\\frac{2}{5}>\\frac{17}{9}.$\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440396 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186440391 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440396 #edb/ref 1069 "q-134232" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440396 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440396 #edb/ref 1071 "Carla has $12$ packages of almonds, each weighing $42$ ounces. In total, Carla has$\\,${{field-1}}\n$\\,$ounces of almonds." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440396 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b88dbf04-dd80-54e8-8fde-e4783f40c410" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440396 #edb/ref 1074 "To calculate the total amount of almonds, we need to multiply $12$ by $42.$\n\nSo, we write it this way:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{aligned} &  &  &  &  \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  &  &  &  & \\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we multiply the ones:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{aligned} &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned} \\\\ \\boxed{0}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned} & \\,\\, & \\begin{aligned}12\\times 2=24\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we multiply the tens:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{aligned} &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned}\\boxed{} \\\\ \\boxed{0}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned} & \\,\\, & \\begin{aligned}12\\times 40=480\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we add the results:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{aligned} &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned}\\boxed{} \\\\ \\boxed{0}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,+\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned} & \\,\\, & \\begin{aligned}24+480=504\\end{aligned} \\\\  & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $12\\times 42=504.$\n\nTherefore, Carla has $504$ ounces of almonds in total." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440397 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186440387 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440397 #edb/ref 1069 "q-134234" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440397 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440397 #edb/ref 1071 "Robert bought $3$ pounds of walnuts while Susan bought $\\frac{17}{9}$ of a pound of walnuts. How many walnuts did they buy together in total? Express your final answer as a mixed number.\n\nThey bought$\\,$ \n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$pounds of walnuts in total." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440397 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c1e68ffc-d3ca-5167-817a-cc856c132a73" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440397 #edb/ref 1074 "To determine the number of walnuts Robert and Susan bought together in total, we have to calculate the following sum:\n\n$$\n3+\\frac{17}{9}\n$$\n\nTo add $3$ and $\\frac{17}{9},$ we need to express the whole number $3$ as an equivalent fraction with a denominator of $9.$\n\nFirst, we write $3$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{1}\n$$\n\nThen we put this fraction over a denominator of $9$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $9:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{3\\times 9}{1\\times 9}=\\frac{27}{9}\n$$\n\nSo now, we have to find:\n\n$$\n\\frac{27}{9}+\\frac{17}{9}\n$$\n\nTo add two fractions with like denominators, we add the numerators and keep the denominators the same:\n\n$$\n\\frac{27}{9}+\\frac{17}{9}=\\frac{27+17}{9}=\\frac{44}{9}\n$$\n\nExpressing our answer as a mixed number, we have\n\n$$\n44÷9=4\\,\\text{R}\\,8=4\\,\\frac{8}{9}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, they bought $4\\,\\frac{8}{9}$ pounds of walnuts." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440820 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186440828 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440820 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045461 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440820 #edb/ref 1045 "Adding, Subtracting, and Multiplying Fractions and Whole Numbers" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440820 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186440828 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440820 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "5b3139fe-03ef-533c-853d-b42018e6a5df" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440821 #edb/ref 1080 "63" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440821 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440821 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "aa74e7d9-ffc5-5c97-8dc2-f332b10ca40e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440822 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186440821 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440822 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440822 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440822 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186440821 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440822 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "2d8f2ec5-b71f-5d9a-9769-6efad96a0b69" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440823 #edb/ref 1088 #edb/ref 17592186048825 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440823 #edb/ref 1088 #edb/ref 17592186048924 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440823 #edb/ref 1088 #edb/ref 17592186049143 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440823 #edb/ref 1088 #edb/ref 17592186049253 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440823 #edb/ref 1088 #edb/ref 17592186049640 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440823 #edb/ref 1088 #edb/ref 17592186049752 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440823 #edb/ref 1088 #edb/ref 17592186050462 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440823 #edb/ref 1089 #edb/ref 17592186440824 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440823 #edb/ref 1090 #uuid "4826a3e8-3156-5563-94b8-6a75cf0e9be3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440824 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186440826 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440824 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186440827 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440824 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186440829 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440824 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186440830 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440824 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186440831 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440824 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186440832 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440824 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186440833 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440824 #edb/ref 1085 "Carla is organizing a nut sale." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440824 #edb/ref 1086 #uuid "4eb5ad9b-c2e0-5a7d-86b8-66d4facf3608" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440824 #edb/ref 1087 #edb/ref 17592186440830 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440825 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186440822 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440825 #edb/ref 1069 "q-134233" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440825 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440825 #edb/ref 1071 "Carla wants to distribute all the almonds into packages weighing $8$ ounces each. Therefore, Carla will be able to make$\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$packages of almonds." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440825 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ea3c1d03-99bf-511e-b905-8e6710f50246" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440825 #edb/ref 1074 "We know that Carla has $504$ ounces of almonds in total. Since each package must contain $8$ ounces of almonds, then she will be able to make $504÷8$ packages.\n\nWe start by writing down the question using long division notation:\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"150\">\n<table align=\"left\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#EBF5FB\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$8$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"2\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$\\,\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{5\\,\\phantom{|}0\\,\\phantom{|}4}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</td>\n<td align=\"left\" width=\"250\">\n<table align=\"left\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr height=\"20\">\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nNotice that $5$ (the number of hundreds in $504$) is less than the divisor $8.$ Therefore, we should consider both the hundreds and tens:\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"150\">\n<table align=\"left\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#EBF5FB\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#EBF5FB\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$6$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#EBF5FB\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$8$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$\\,\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{5\\,\\phantom{|}0\\,\\phantom{|}4}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$-\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$4$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$8$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$↓$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</td>\n<td align=\"left\" width=\"250\">\n<table align=\"left\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr height=\"20\">\n<td>Divide: $50÷8=6\\,\\text{R}\\,2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr height=\"20\">\n<td>Write over tens: $6$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr height=\"25\">\n<td>Multiply: $8\\times 6=48$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr height=\"20\">\n<td>Subtract: $50-48=2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr height=\"25\">\n<td>Bring down: $4$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nNext, we consider the ones:\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"150\">\n<table align=\"left\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#EBF5FB\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#EBF5FB\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$6$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#EBF5FB\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#EBF5FB\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$8$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$\\,\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{5\\,\\phantom{|}0\\,\\phantom{|}4}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$-\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$4$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$8$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$-\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$\\boxed{\\text{0}}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</td>\n<td align=\"left\" width=\"250\">\n<table align=\"left\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr height=\"20\">\n<td>Divide: $24÷8=3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr height=\"20\">\n<td>Write over ones: $3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr height=\"25\">\n<td>Multiply: $8\\times 3=24$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr height=\"20\">\n<td>Subtract: $24-24=0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nWe get the remainder $0$, so the division is done.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n504÷8=63.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440826 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186440827 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440826 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0216fd09-2f2b-54b3-bef2-5a231a977b91" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440826 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186440397 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440827 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186440829 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440827 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1a94c276-640b-545e-a04c-e330a5a242bc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440827 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186440393 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440828 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "232eb213-9c5a-5ee6-97f2-100765f33e43" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440828 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186440823 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440829 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186440832 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440829 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "624c9ceb-59e5-5d35-98c1-a18ab719dec0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440829 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186440395 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440830 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186440831 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440830 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "72323577-5a50-54c6-a65d-bc25ba76f581" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440830 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186440392 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440831 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186440833 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440831 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a8cab5b1-8ff1-5abd-bce3-91aa26f135b2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440831 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186440396 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440832 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d2ca046e-ab9a-51fa-b981-4cfc39b51456" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440832 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186440394 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440833 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186440826 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440833 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d46adb27-6432-5d9a-9f53-f2a56cd17e79" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440833 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186440825 #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
